  "chapter_summary": "This chapter introduces the precise physical definitions of work, energy, and power, distinguishing them from their everyday usage. It begins with the scalar product of vectors as a mathematical prerequisite. The work-energy theorem is derived, establishing that the change in kinetic energy equals the work done by the net force. The chapter explores work done by constant and variable forces, defining work as the area under a force-displacement curve. It introduces potential energy for conservative forces (gravity and springs) and the principle of conservation of mechanical energy. Finally, it covers the concept of power and applies conservation laws to analyze elastic and inelastic collisions in one and two dimensions.",
